 \begin{abstract}
-Geometry-conditioned multi-view diffusion supplies images for 3D texturing, but stronger conditioning and improved image agreement need not yield faithful baked appearance. We study Timestep-Conditioned Adapter Scaling (TCAS) on an existing GeoTex-Adapter residual path, retaining C3 as a historically selected middle-high profile. On 300 input-disjoint Fresh C objects, C3 improves FG-PSNR over Fixed Low by 0.52 dB (95\% object-bootstrap CI $[0.40,0.64]$), while Fixed High and Generic Linear have better means on all four image endpoints. This contrast changes both strength and timing. A same-cohort six-condition addendum, comprising four equal-applied-budget controls and two separate strength controls, finds C3 below Fixed Mean and Late-high by 0.13 and 0.56 dB FG-PSNR, respectively, with matching losses on the other image endpoints. On ten previously unbaked, compatibility-selected objects, calibrated native-UV baking gives Late-high--Fixed Mean unseen FG-LPIPS $-0.00265$ ($[-0.00414,-0.00137]$), favorable on all ten. Substantial source-appearance errors remain, limiting this result to relative metric improvement. A separate fifty-object native MV-Adapter comparison gives Middle-high--Fixed Mean FG-PSNR $+0.029$ dB ($[-0.019,+0.081]$). These results characterize conditioning-strength allocation in the tested graphics system and its interface-dependent limits. They support neither a generally preferred middle window nor general cross-backbone or PBR fidelity claims; temporal scheduling itself is established prior work.
