 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
 
-Multi-view diffusion systems generate target-view images that can later be projected or baked onto a 3D surface. In geometry-conditioned systems, an adapter injects shape information into the denoiser through intermediate residuals. The strength applied to those residuals is therefore a practical inference-time control: changing it can alter how strongly geometry conditions the generated views and can also change their appearance. The useful setting depends on the adapter implementation and on the image endpoint being considered, so a single structural score cannot characterize the full effect.
-
-This paper studies that control question in a fixed MVPainter-style multi-view texturing pipeline. Timestep-Conditioned Adapter Scaling (TCAS) applies a piecewise requested-scale profile to the existing GeoTex-Adapter residual path without training parameters or changing model weights. The formulation records three separate quantities: the scale requested for a denoising step and layer group, the scale actually applied after native wrapper caps, and the norm of the scaled residual correction. This makes the implementation and the scope of each comparison measurable.
-
-Layer- and timestep-dependent control is established prior work. Scheduled Style Injection (SSI) varies StyleID's attention-mixing parameter across decoder layers and denoising steps, and also schedules geometry-conditioned ControlNet strength along those axes. We therefore do not present TCAS as a new general scheduling primitive. Our contribution is an empirical graphics-system characterization of a specific residual path: we measure how requests pass through native caps, how the applied corrections vary, and how fixed and scheduled controls compare on paired multi-view image endpoints. SSI's style-transfer results do not determine the results of these object-level texturing comparisons; differences in task and interface require measurement here, but do not make the underlying scheduling idea new.
