 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
-
-This study treats geometry-adapter strength as an observable inference control in a multi-view texturing pipeline. TCAS specifies a requested temporal profile on the existing GeoTex-Adapter path; C3, $(1.25,2.50,1.25)$, is the recorded profile evaluated here, not a general scheduling rule.
-
-Fresh C supports a registered C3--GFL FG-PSNR gain, but stronger fixed and linear baselines perform better and that contrast changes applied strength as well as timing. The original pooled headline is not a numerical replication target for this retrained checkpoint.
-
-The four equal-budget controls within the six-condition 300-object matrix do not support a generally preferred middle window: C3 trails Fixed Mean and Late-high on all four image endpoints. In the calibrated ten-object bake pilot, Late-high improves unseen FG-LPIPS over Fixed Mean by 0.00265, favorable on all ten; this is a small relative metric result on compatible unlit assets. C3--GFH PSNR is not stably positive and SSIM is lower. Broad image/bake rank agreement remains positive, while the LPIPS winner changes on seven objects. The fifty-object native MV-Adapter primary does not separate Middle-high from Fixed Mean. These complete results support controlled empirical conclusions and interface/endpoint limits, not C3 optimality, a new scheduling primitive or general cross-backbone transfer. Seam quality, PBR fidelity, a validated selector and the full FAC training replay remain unresolved.
+TCAS provides a training-free stage-dependent control for geometry-adapter strength in multi-view texture generation. The C3 profile and original development and preference studies retain their historical identity. A matched Fresh C evaluation supports C3 over Fixed Low, while strong fixed/linear controls and equal-budget allocation comparisons delimit that benefit. Calibrated unseen-view baking supplies a small named Late-high LPIPS result, and native MV-Adapter testing evaluates the same stage question on a second interface without a separated primary gain. These findings establish a concrete, reproducible control-and-evaluation study for the tested texturing system; they do not imply a universal middle-stage optimum or resolved reference-appearance fidelity.
 
 \section*{Data availability}
 
